\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The completion matches the planar gasket}
\date{2026-11-07}

\p{Let #{\pi} and #{K} be the planar coding and its image in
\ref{fdip-00EO}, and let #{\pi_X} and #{X} be the resistance
coding and its image in \ref{fdip-00EQ}. Two words satisfy
#{\pi_X(\omega)=\pi_X(\tau)} if and only if
#{\pi(\omega)=\pi(\tau)}. The induced bijection from #{K}
onto #{X} is a homeomorphism.}

\proof{
\p{Junction words agree in both codings, by
\ref{fdip-00EO} and \ref{fdip-00EQ}. If the words are not
equal and not a junction pair, \ref{fdip-00EO} gives
#{\delta=\bigl|\pi(\omega)-\pi(\tau)\bigr|>0}. Choose #{n}
with #{4\cdot 2^{-n}<\delta}. Let #{U} be the union of the
planar #{n}-cells that meet the closed ball of radius
#{2^{-n}} about #{\pi(\omega)}, and let #{W} be the same
union about #{\pi(\tau)}. A point of a cell meeting such a
ball lies at distance at most #{2\cdot 2^{-n}} from the
center. A common point of #{U} and #{W} would lie at most
that far from each center, so the centers would be at most
#{4\cdot 2^{-n}} apart, which is less than #{\delta}. The
two unions share no point and no vertex of #{V_n}.
Define #{\rho} on #{V_n} by the value #{1} on vertices in
#{U} and the value #{0} on the remaining vertices. The cell
of #{\omega} meets the first ball, so its three corners
have value #{1}. The cell of #{\tau} has value #{0} on its
corners.}

\p{For #{m>n}, every edge of #{G_m} lies inside some
#{n}-cell. On an #{n}-cell whose corners have one common
value, the constant extension contributes nothing to the
energy, while any other value on a later vertex of that
cell produces a positive edge term. The energy-minimizing
extension is therefore equal to #{1} on every vertex of the
cell of #{\omega} and equal to #{0} on every vertex of the
cell of #{\tau}. \ref{fdip-00DT} keeps the energy equal to
#{E_n(\rho)}. The graph #{G_n} is connected and both values
occur, so some edge contributes
#{\bigl(\frac{5}{3}\bigr)^n} and #{E_n(\rho)>0}. The
extension lies in #{F_*}.
\citet{Proposition 3.6}{kigami2010resistance} yields
##{\bigl|u(a)-u(b)\bigr|^2\le R(a,b)\,E_*(u)}
on #{V_*}, so #{u} is uniformly continuous and extends to
#{X}. Vertices of the cell of #{\omega} converge to
#{\pi_X(\omega)} and all carry the value #{1}, while the
cell of #{\tau} carries #{0}. Hence
##{R\bigl(\pi_X(\omega),\pi_X(\tau)\bigr)\ge 1/E_n(\rho)>0.}}

\p{The two codings have the same fibers. The set of infinite
words is compact, and #{\pi} is a continuous surjection
onto the Hausdorff space #{K}, hence a closed quotient map.
A map out of #{K} is continuous when its composition with
#{\pi} is continuous, so #{\pi_X} induces a continuous map
#{\varphi:K\to X}. The separation makes #{\varphi}
bijective. A continuous bijection from a compact space to a
Hausdorff space is a homeomorphism.}
}
